// deem_incr_static_retract_e2e — P2: `min`/`max` UNDER RETRACTION, and the
// multiplicity map P1 deleted on purpose.
//
// ── WHY THERE IS A MAP AT ALL, AND WHY A FIXTURE CANNOT SEE IT BY ACCIDENT ──
// `count` and `sum` have INVERSES: retract a row and subtract what it added.
// `min` and `max` do not. Retract the current minimum and the new minimum is a
// fact about EVERY OTHER ROW STILL PRESENT, which a single cached extremum
// cannot answer. So a retract-eligible `min`/`max` carries a per-(group,
// aggregate) value→live-multiplicity map beside its cached best, and rescans
// that map's live keys when the best itself is retracted — the shape the
// dynamic interpreter has had since ADR 0013 §3.1, which P1 deleted with a
// comment saying P2 would put it back.
//
// ⚠ EVERY BLOCK BELOW IS A CELL A SIMPLER FIXTURE CANNOT REACH, and that is the
// selection rule for what is here. In particular:
//
//   • inserting each value ONCE cannot see multiplicity at all. Block B inserts
//     7 TWICE and retracts it ONCE; the answer must still be 7. A map that
//     stored presence instead of a count answers 3.
//   • two rows on the same side of 2^63 cannot see the ORDER. Block A retracts
//     2^64−1 out of {5, 2^63, 2^64−1}; the answer is 2^63. A signed comparison
//     answers 5, because 2^63 read as i64 is negative.
//   • a group that never EMPTIES cannot see the liveness filter or the stale
//     best. Blocks C and A5 empty one and then insert into it again.
//   • a float pair that is not {−0.0, +0.0} cannot see the map's KEY. Block D
//     retracts −0.0 out of {−0.0, +0.0, NaN, 1.0}: the emitted `min` must become
//     +0.0. A map keyed on `f64_data_key` — which maps both zeros onto ONE key —
//     leaves the count at 1, retracts nothing, and answers −0.0.
//
// ── THE THREE WITNESSES ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// "Both engines agree" is not evidence: this arc has measured a three-way
// differential passing for months while all three sides ran the same wrong
// fold. So every number here is asserted against
//   (i)   a HAND-DERIVED constant, written from the input arithmetic;
//   (ii)  the static BATCH fn `<q>()` over the SURVIVING multiset — a fold that
//         shares with the retract path only the argument-expression emission;
//   (iii) the DYNAMIC interpreter's incremental engine driven with a w = −1
//         `ZBatch` (blocks A and B) — a genuinely separate implementation over
//         `RtVal`, and a WASTING one: P5 deletes it.
//
// ── THE HAND-DERIVED TABLE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A. u64 column, one group k=0, rows in order 5, 2^63, 2^64−1.
//      after epoch      : c=3  mn=5              mx=18446744073709551615
//      retract 2^64−1   : c=2  mn=5              mx=9223372036854775808   (=2^63)
//      retract 2^63     : c=1  mn=5              mx=5
//      retract 5        : the group is EMPTY — 0 rows in the snapshot
//      insert 7         : c=1  mn=7              mx=7
//        (mn=7 is the SEED GATE. The cell still holds 5 from before the group
//         emptied; 5 is present in no row, and comparing against it answers 5.)
// B. i64 column, one group k=0, rows 7, 7, 3.
//      after epoch      : c=3  t=17  lo=3  hi=7
//      retract one 7    : c=2  t=10  lo=3  hi=7    ← multiplicity: 7 SURVIVES
//      retract the 7    : c=1  t=3   lo=3  hi=3
// C. i64, rows (k=0,v=1) (k=1,v=2); retract (k=0,v=1) → ONE row, key 1.
// D. f64, one group, rows −0.0, +0.0, NaN, 1.0 in that order.
//      `f64_data_key` is the order: −0.0 == +0.0, every NaN is one value and is
//      the GREATEST. The improve is strict, so the first of a tie wins.
//      after epoch      : lo = −0.0 (bits 0x8000000000000000)
//                         hi = NaN  (bits 0x7FF8000000000000)
//      retract −0.0     : lo = +0.0 (bits 0x0000000000000000)
//      retract NaN      : hi = 1.0  (bits 0x3FF0000000000000)
//
// ⚠⚠ THIS BLOCK ASSERTS SEMANTICS VICTOR HAS ALREADY DECIDED TO CHANGE
// (2026-08-07). It is GREEN, and its green must not be read as settled.
// TODAY identity and order are two different functions: the map's KEY is
// `f64_to_bits` (bitwise, so −0.0 and +0.0 are two entries and every NaN
// payload is its own entry) while the COMPARATOR is `f64_data_key` (the
// FP-as-DATA order, which calls them equal). That split contradicts this
// project's own recorded rule — THE RULE LIVES IN THE KEY.
// DECIDED, NOT YET DONE: PostgreSQL semantics all the way into the key —
// **all zeros are ONE element, all NaNs are ONE element**. Scheduled after
// P3; see the memory note `design_fp_as_data_postgres_semantics`.
// WHEN IT LANDS, THESE ASSERTIONS INVERT AND THAT IS THE POINT: the two
// zeros become one entry of multiplicity 2, so retracting −0.0 leaves a
// zero standing and `lo` reads 0x8000000000000000 (the first arrival), NOT
// 0x0000000000000000 as asserted at :332. Likewise the NaN retraction stops
// depending on the payload. A reader who "fixes" the emitter to match the
// decision MUST update :332 — the red is expected, not a regression.
// A6. THE HALF OF u64's ORDER BLOCK A CANNOT SEE. Block A only ever retracts
//    the current MAX, so `mn` sits at 5 from the first row and is never
//    RE-DERIVED: a signed comparator in the re-MIN rescan goes unnoticed there.
//      a. retract 2^63 (NEITHER extremum) → c=2, mn=5, mx=2^64−1 unmoved.
//      b. fresh handle, retract 5 (the MINIMUM) → mn must be re-derived over
//         the two survivors {2^63, 2^64−1} and the answer is 2^63. A SIGNED
//         rescan answers 2^64−1, whose i64 payload is −1. Two survivors is the
//         minimum that can discriminate: with one, the loop returns it whatever
//         the comparator is.
// E. a u64 `sum` driven BELOW ZERO must ERR, not wrap, AND SAY `Underflow`.
//    `el_addu` had no `el_subu` sibling until this arc, and `a - b` on a u64
//    cell wraps to ≈2^64 — or traps outright — so the fault was never absent,
//    only unreportable. Insert 1 TWICE (count 2, sum 2), then retract a row
//    carrying 5: the count goes 2 → 1, which is LEGAL, so the group-count guard
//    does not fire first and the arithmetic is reached. (Insert-once-retract-
//    twice would return at the count guard and this witness would evaporate.)
// F. retracting into a group that never existed → `Err(RetractAbsent)`.
// G. retracting a row twice → the group's count would go below zero →
//    `Err(RetractAbsent)`.
// G2. the group EXISTS but the VALUE never did — insert (0,3) twice, retract
//    (0,8). The count 2 → 1 is legal, so only the map can catch it, and the
//    map's `remove` of a missing key is a silent no-op. DECIDED, not measured:
//    neither tier had an answer, and it is the same caller mistake as F and G,
//    so it gets the same name.
//
// ⚠ EVERY ERROR ASSERTION NAMES ITS ARM. `Result::Err(_e) => {}` would pass on
// any arm, and the arms exist precisely because `Overflow` used to be returned
// for two different mistakes.
//
// ── EXIT CODES: THE CEILING IS A NUMBER THE PROGRAM REFUSES TO RUN WITHOUT ──
// `.expected` says `exit: 0`, so a non-zero code is a DIAGNOSTIC naming the
// assertion that fired. 126, 127 and 128+signal belong to the shell, so a code
// up there is one number standing for two states — which is how this very
// fixture's ancestor had a 132 that meant both "block D's second float check"
// and "SIGILL", disambiguated only because the harness printed the signal text.
// (Its header claimed "All ≤ 122" while using 130-162: the header lied too.)
//
// ⚠ AND UNTIL 2026-08-07 THE FIX FOR THAT LIE WAS ITSELF ONLY A SENTENCE. This
// block said "EVERY ONE ≤ 117" and nothing refused a 118th, a 130th or a 200th:
// the ceiling lived in a comment, which is the same form as the claim it
// replaced. `check_layout` and `main`'s chokepoint below are that sentence as a
// mechanism — `TOP_CODE` is DECLARED and checked against `EXIT_MAX` on every
// run, and the value `assertions()` actually returns is checked against
// `TOP_CODE` before it leaves the process. A code above the ceiling exits
// `EXIT_OVERRUN` and prints itself instead of impersonating a signal.
// (`deem_incr_diff_harness` carries the same two guards, derived independently
// from the same 8-bit fact.)
//
// 117 distinct assertions do not fit in 10-decade blocks under that ceiling, so:
// codes 1-12 are the BLOCKS' PLUMBING (a call that must succeed returned `Err`
// — A is 1, A6 is 2, … (iii) is 12), and every genuine ASSERTION gets its own
// code in source order:
//     A 13-28 · A6 29-39 · B 40-53 · C 54-57 · D 58-64 · E 65-68 · F 69-72
//     G 73-76 · G2 77-83 · H 84-91 · (ii) 92-102 · (iii) 103-117
//     the `SrcUnknown` arms of E/F/G/G2, appended: 118-121
//     G2's transactionality pair 122 · G3 123 (arms) and 124 (state)
// One code, one assertion — WITH THE 2026-08-08 EXCEPTION THIS HEADER ITSELF
// PREDICTED: 122, 123 and 124 each carry SEVERAL assertions and separate them on
// STDOUT via `println!`, because those are the last three numbers under the
// ceiling and the alternative was renumbering 121 codes. Block E's 66/67/68 were
// re-cut at the same time (E changed arm — see the block) and 66 likewise names
// itself. A reader chasing a failure code in that band reads the printed line,
// not the number alone.
// ⚠ WITH ONE GAP, NAMED RATHER THAN LEFT FOR A READER TO FIND: **7 IS RESERVED
// AND NEVER EMITTED.** It is block F's plumbing slot, and F has no plumbing —
// its only call is the `iq_retract` that IS the assertion, so there is no
// must-succeed call left over to fail. The slot is kept rather than closed
// because closing it would renumber 110 codes to reclaim one, and every control
// revert recorded against this file would land somewhere else. A reader who
// counts 1-12 and finds eleven is looking at this, not at a deleted assertion.
//
// THE GUARDS ARE PROVEN TO BITE — five controls, each predicted then reverted:
//   · `TOP_CODE` 117 → 132, i.e. a 13th block declaring itself into SIGILL's
//     number → exit 125 before any assertion runs, "declared top 132 exceeds
//     the ceiling 120".
//   · the last assertion made to return 130 without bumping `TOP_CODE` → exit
//     125, "an assertion returned 130 > TOP_CODE 117". At `a484078c` that same
//     edit exited 130, which is 128+SIGINT.
//   · `EXIT_MAX` 120 → 125    → 125, "ceiling 125 collides with the guard 125".
//   · `EXIT_OVERRUN` 125 → 130 → 125 (NOT 130), "guard 130 is itself in the
//     shell/signal band" — that branch returns a hard literal precisely because
//     it fires when `EXIT_OVERRUN` is the thing that is wrong.
//   · THE CONTROL, because a chokepoint that swallows everything is not a
//     chokepoint: the same assertion broken at its OWN code still exits 117.
//     The guard narrows nothing.
//
// ── THE OPPOSITE HOLE — A STATE WITH NO CODE — CLOSED 2026-08-07 ────────────
// The guards above are about ONE CODE STANDING FOR TWO STATES. The inverse ran
// open until this slice: `.expected` was `exit: 0` with NO `stdout:` key, and
// `run_test.sh:217` compares stdout only when the key is present, so an
// assertion that STOPPED RUNNING was invisible — delete one, the file still
// exits 0 and nothing moves. `deem_incr_diff_harness` had already solved this
// (`spine ok ep=5 cmp=20 cells=300`, pinned) and this file had not.
// It does now: `main` prints
//     retract ok chk=106 rows=28
// on the success path and `.expected` pins that line. Both numbers are DERIVED —
// `chk` is bumped inside `bad`, the chokepoint all 89 predicates below pass
// through, plus `arm_ok` in the four refusal blocks' accepting arm; `rows`
// accumulates the `.len()` each snapshot actually returned. Neither is a literal
// typed into a comment, which is the form this header records failing twice.
// PROVEN TO BITE, predicted then reverted:
//   · delete the A6b re-MIN assertion at `s6b.get(0i64).2 != 2^63` → the run
//     still exits 0 and prints `chk=92`; `run_test.sh` says `FAIL: stdout
//     mismatch` and the test is red on a run that used to be silent.
//   · delete the WHOLE A6b block, its batch witness with it — the only place the
//     re-MIN rescan over two surviving u64 values is ever read — → `chk=86
//     rows=23`, seven predicates and two snapshots gone. BOTH axes move,
//     because a deleted block stops a snapshot being TAKEN and not merely
//     asserted, and that is why there are two axes rather than one.
// ⚠ WHAT IT STILL DOES NOT COVER, stated at the mechanism as well as here: a
// census sees an assertion that stops RUNNING, not one WEAKENED IN PLACE.
// MEASURED 2026-08-07: with the emitted f64 `min` improve inverted this file
// exits 59 and prints NOTHING, because its assertions carry no `println!` — the
// code is the whole channel on the FAILURE path, and the census does not change
// that (it prints only when `r == 0`). That is sound while codes stay unique
// (they are, 1..121 with 7 reserved), but it leaves no room: 122/123/124 are the
// only free codes under the ceiling, so the 4th assertion added here forces the
// parameter channel rather than merely suggesting it.
//
// ⚠ 118-121 SIT OUT OF SEQUENCE ON PURPOSE, and the ceiling moved 117 → 121
// rather than the arms sharing a code. `ElError` grew a fifth variant when the
// source tag became strict (P3), so every four-arm `match` in this file has a
// new arm; each is "the dispatch stopped reading its own tag" at a DIFFERENT
// call, which is four facts and not one. 121 < 125 keeps every code clear of
// the shell's 126/127/128+signal, which is what the ceiling is actually for —
// the number 117 was never the constraint, 125 is.
//
// exit 0 = the static tier retracts, at u64's extrema, with multiplicity, with
//          identity preserved on floats, and refuses — by name — to retract
//          what was never inserted.
package test;

use logos.lang.writ.container;
use logos.lang.writ.anyval;
use logos.lang.writ.array;
use logos.std.wql.ir;
use logos.std.wql.catalog_macro;
use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.std.wql.el;    // ElError — the error ARM is asserted by name, not by `_`
use logos.std.fmt;       // println! — the ceiling guard names the offending code
use logos.mem.deem;
use logos.lang.result;
use logos.lang.num.float;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

struct U { pub k: i64, pub v: u64 }
struct I { pub k: i64, pub v: i64 }
struct F { pub k: i64, pub v: f64 }

pub schema UR : code(0x0E71_0000_0000_0001) { k: i64 = 0, v: u64 = 1 }
pub schema IR : code(0x0E71_0000_0000_0002) { k: i64 = 0, v: i64 = 1 }
resource cat = schema_catalog!{ UR, IR };

// A. u64 min/max — NO `sum` in this list on purpose: 5 + 2^63 + (2^64−1)
// overflows u64, and this block is about the ORDER, not about the addition.
pub deem uq(src: &[U]) { from src s
      group by s.k
      aggregate c = count(), mn = min(s.v), mx = max(s.v)
      select (key, c, mn, mx) }

// B/C. i64 — the control column, with both invertible algebras beside the
// semilattice ones so that a fix to one is visible against the others.
pub deem iq(src: &[I]) { from src s
      group by s.k
      aggregate c = count(), t = sum(s.v), lo = min(s.v), hi = max(s.v)
      select (key, c, t, lo, hi) }

// D. f64 min/max — IN SCOPE for retraction and `sum`/`avg` over a float is NOT
// (see `incr_retract_eligible`): an extremum is a value SELECTED from the
// multiset, so nothing rounds, while an f64 sum's inverse rests on
// (S+x)−x == S, which is an open fork.
pub deem fq(src: &[F]) { from src s
      group by s.k
      aggregate lo = min(s.v), hi = max(s.v)
      select (key, lo, hi) }

// E. the u64 SUM, on its own, so its underflow is not entangled with block A.
pub deem sq(src: &[U]) { from src s
      group by s.k
      aggregate c = count(), t = sum(s.v)
      select (key, c, t) }

// G3b. THE MINIMUM SHAPE THAT CAN STILL HOLD A WRONG NUMBER: one `sum`, no
// `count`, no `min`/`max` — so the handle carries the group key, the group count
// and ONE accumulator, and no value-level state whatsoever. This is the query
// the 2026-08-08 defect was reported on.
pub deem tq(src: &[I]) { from src s
      group by s.k
      aggregate t = sum(s.v)
      select (key, t) }

fn u1(k: i64, v: u64) -> [U; 1] { return [U { k: k, v: v }]; }
fn i1(k: i64, v: i64) -> [I; 1] { return [I { k: k, v: v }]; }
fn f1(k: i64, v: f64) -> [F; 1] { return [F { k: k, v: v }]; }

fn mkU(d: &Writ, k: i64, v: u64) -> WAny {
    let r: UR = d.make::<UR>(); r.k = k; r.v = v;
    return WAny::ref_to((r.m as i64) as *const u8);
}
fn mkI(d: &Writ, k: i64, v: i64) -> WAny {
    let r: IR = d.make::<IR>(); r.k = k; r.v = v;
    return WAny::ref_to((r.m as i64) as *const u8);
}

// ── THE CEILING, AS A MECHANISM ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// `TOP_CODE` is the highest code the assertions below may return. It is a
// DECLARATION, so `check_layout` can refuse a ceiling this file has outgrown
// before a single assertion runs; and `main` checks the value `assertions()`
// ACTUALLY returns against it, so a block appended after (iii) that marches
// past 117 is caught on the number rather than on the declaration.
// ⚠ 121 → 124 ON 2026-08-08, AND THE HEADROOM IS NOW GONE — SAY SO RATHER THAN
// LEAVE IT TO BE DISCOVERED. G2's two transactionality assertions took 122, G3's
// refusal arms took 123 and G3's state assertions took 124, each sharing its
// code with a `println!` that names which one fired. That is the PARAMETER
// CHANNEL the exit-code header above predicted would be forced, and it is
// forced: the next assertion added to this file has no number left and must
// either share one of 122/123/124 or the file must be split.
fn TOP_CODE()     -> i32 { return 124i32; }
// 125 is the guard; 126/127/128+N belong to the shell.
// ⚠ MERGE RESOLUTION 2026-08-07, and it was PROVEN LIVE rather than assumed:
// P3 appended the four `SrcUnknown` arms at 118-121 while this guard arrived
// from a sibling commit pre-set to TOP_CODE 117 / EXIT_MAX 120. The merge was
// textually clean and SEMANTICALLY WRONG — four codes above the declared top,
// green only because no arm fires today. Raising TOP_CODE to 121 ALONE was
// tried first, on purpose: every run then exits 125 with `declared top 121
// exceeds the ceiling 120`, which is the guard refusing a space it cannot
// represent. EXIT_MAX had to move with it. A resolution that only edited the
// comment would have left the fixture green and the ceiling a lie again.
fn EXIT_MAX()     -> i32 { return 124i32; }
fn EXIT_OVERRUN() -> i32 { return 125i32; }

// ── THE CENSUS: THE OPPOSITE HOLE TO THE CEILING, AND IT IS NOW A MECHANISM ──
// The ceiling above is about ONE CODE STANDING FOR TWO STATES. This is the
// inverse: A STATE WITH NO CODE. `.expected` used to be `exit: 0` with no
// `stdout:` key at all, and `run_test.sh` compares stdout only when the key is
// present — so an assertion that STOPPED RUNNING was invisible. Delete one, the
// file still exits 0, nothing moves. Every guard in this file watches the value
// a failing run reports; none of them watched whether the run happened.
//
// `Tally` is that missing half. `chk` counts ASSERTION SITES THAT EXECUTED and
// `rows` counts ROWS THE ENGINE ACTUALLY HANDED BACK, and `main` prints both on
// the success path where `.expected` pins them. The numbers are DERIVED — `bad`
// is the chokepoint every one of the 89 predicates below passes through, and
// `rows` accumulates the `.len()` of each snapshot at the site that produced it
// — because a count written into a comment, or into `.expected` by hand, is the
// very form this file already records as having failed twice (the header's
// "All ≤ 122" and "EVERY ONE ≤ 117").
//
// ⚠ TWO AXES OF DIFFERENT KIND, ON PURPOSE. `chk` counts SYNTAX that ran; `rows`
// counts DATA that came back. Deleting one predicate moves `chk` alone; deleting
// a BLOCK stops a snapshot being taken at all and moves BOTH. That is the
// difference between "an assertion was dropped" and "a call to the mechanism was
// dropped", and one number cannot tell them apart.
//
// PINNED: `retract ok chk=106 rows=28`. 106 = 98 `if` predicates + 8 accepting
// arms taken through `arm_ok` (E's refusal, E2's `el_subu` borrow, F, G, G2, G3,
// G3b, G3c); 28 = the rows the 24 snapshots, 3 batch recomputes and 3
// interpreter snapshots handed back — `assertions` has no loop, so each site
// runs exactly once and the census is a fixed number. HAND-DERIVED FIRST AND
// MEASURED SECOND, and they agreed; a disagreement would have been evidence
// about the code, not a licence to write down whatever printed.
// (2026-08-08: 93/25 → 106/28. +13 checks and +3 snapshots from block E's
// re-sited `el_subu` witness, G2's two transactionality cells and the three G3
// blocks. Hand-derived to 106/28 before the run, and the run agreed.)
//
// ⚠ WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER, NAMED AT THE MECHANISM RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE
// FOUND: a census sees an assertion that stops RUNNING, not one that stops
// ASSERTING. Rewriting `!= 5u64` into `!= a0.get(0i64).2` still calls `bad` once
// and still reads the same rows, so `chk` and `rows` are unmoved. That failure
// belongs to review, not to this counter.
struct Tally { pub chk: i64, pub rows: i64 }

// THE CHOKEPOINT. Every predicate in `assertions` goes through here, so the
// printed census is derived from the checks that actually ran and not from
// whoever remembered to bump a number. The return value is the predicate's,
// unchanged — `bad(t, X)` and `X` decide identically.
fn bad(t: &mut Tally, c: bool) -> bool { t.chk = t.chk + 1i64; return c; }

// The same bump for the four REFUSAL blocks (E, F, G, G2), whose assertion is a
// `match` and not an `if`: it fires in the arm that MUST be taken, so a deleted
// refusal block lowers `chk` exactly like a deleted `if`.
fn arm_ok(t: &mut Tally) { t.chk = t.chk + 1i64; }

fn check_layout() -> i32 {
    if TOP_CODE() > EXIT_MAX() {
        println!("EXIT-CODE LAYOUT: declared top {} exceeds the ceiling {}", TOP_CODE(), EXIT_MAX());
        return EXIT_OVERRUN();
    }
    if EXIT_MAX() >= EXIT_OVERRUN() {
        println!("EXIT-CODE LAYOUT: ceiling {} collides with the guard {}", EXIT_MAX(), EXIT_OVERRUN());
        return EXIT_OVERRUN();
    }
    // ⚠ A HARD 125, NOT `EXIT_OVERRUN()`: this branch fires exactly when
    // `EXIT_OVERRUN` has been edited into the band it exists to stay out of, so
    // reporting through it would report the defect using the defect.
    if EXIT_OVERRUN() > 125i32 {
        println!("EXIT-CODE LAYOUT: guard {} is itself in the shell/signal band", EXIT_OVERRUN());
        return 125i32;
    }
    return 0i32;
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    let lay: i32 = check_layout();
    if lay != 0i32 { return lay; }
    let mut t: Tally = Tally { chk: 0i64, rows: 0i64 };
    let r: i32 = assertions(&mut t);
    if r > TOP_CODE() {
        println!("EXIT-CODE OVERRUN: an assertion returned {} > TOP_CODE {}", r, TOP_CODE());
        return EXIT_OVERRUN();
    }
    // THE CENSUS, AND IT IS AN ASSERTION: `.expected` pins this line. It is
    // printed ONLY on the success path, so a failing run still says nothing on
    // stdout and is already red on its code; what this adds is the failure the
    // code channel cannot carry — an assertion that stopped running.
    if r == 0i32 { println!("retract ok chk={} rows={}", t.chk, t.rows); }
    return r;
}

fn assertions(t: &mut Tally) -> i32 {
    // ══ A. u64 min/max under retraction, at the extrema ═══════════════════
    let mut hu: UqIncr = uq_incremental();
    let ua: [U; 3] = [U { k: 0i64, v: 5u64 },
                      U { k: 0i64, v: 9223372036854775808u64 },
                      U { k: 0i64, v: 18446744073709551615u64 }];
    match uq_epoch(&mut hu, &ua) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 1i32; } }
    let a0: Vec<(i64, i64, u64, u64)> = match uq_snapshot(&hu) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 1i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + a0.len();
    if bad(t, a0.len() != 1i64) { return 13i32; }
    if bad(t, a0.get(0i64).1 != 3i64) { return 14i32; }
    if bad(t, a0.get(0i64).2 != 5u64) { return 15i32; }
    if bad(t, a0.get(0i64).3 != 18446744073709551615u64) { return 16i32; }

    // retract 2^64−1. THE DISCRIMINATING CELL: 2^63, not 5.
    let ur1: [U; 1] = u1(0i64, 18446744073709551615u64);
    match uq_retract(&mut hu, &ur1) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 1i32; } }
    let a1: Vec<(i64, i64, u64, u64)> = match uq_snapshot(&hu) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 1i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + a1.len();
    if bad(t, a1.len() != 1i64) { return 17i32; }
    if bad(t, a1.get(0i64).1 != 2i64) { return 18i32; }
    if bad(t, a1.get(0i64).2 != 5u64) { return 19i32; }
    if bad(t, a1.get(0i64).3 != 9223372036854775808u64) { return 20i32; }

    // retract 2^63 → one row left, {5}
    let ur2: [U; 1] = u1(0i64, 9223372036854775808u64);
    match uq_retract(&mut hu, &ur2) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 1i32; } }
    let a2: Vec<(i64, i64, u64, u64)> = match uq_snapshot(&hu) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 1i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + a2.len();
    if bad(t, a2.len() != 1i64) { return 21i32; }
    if bad(t, a2.get(0i64).2 != 5u64) { return 22i32; }
    if bad(t, a2.get(0i64).3 != 5u64) { return 23i32; }

    // retract 5 → THE GROUP IS EMPTY. The slot survives; the ROW must not.
    let ur3: [U; 1] = u1(0i64, 5u64);
    match uq_retract(&mut hu, &ur3) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 1i32; } }
    let a3: Vec<(i64, i64, u64, u64)> = match uq_snapshot(&hu) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 1i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + a3.len();
    if bad(t, a3.len() != 0i64) { return 24i32; }

    // A5. THE SEED GATE, POST-RETRACTION. The cells still hold 5 from the run
    // above — a value now present in no row. `min` over the single row {7} must
    // answer 7; comparing against the stale cell answers 5.
    let ui4: [U; 1] = u1(0i64, 7u64);
    match uq_epoch(&mut hu, &ui4) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 1i32; } }
    let a4: Vec<(i64, i64, u64, u64)> = match uq_snapshot(&hu) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 1i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + a4.len();
    if bad(t, a4.len() != 1i64) { return 25i32; }
    if bad(t, a4.get(0i64).1 != 1i64) { return 26i32; }
    if bad(t, a4.get(0i64).2 != 7u64) { return 27i32; }
    if bad(t, a4.get(0i64).3 != 7u64) { return 28i32; }

    // ══ A6. RE-MIN OVER A SURVIVING MULTI-VALUE SET ═══════════════════════
    // ⚠ THE CELL BLOCK A ABOVE CANNOT REACH, AND IT IS THE HALF OF u64's ORDER
    // THAT WAS NEVER TESTED. Block A only ever retracts the current MAX, so
    // `mn` sits at 5 from the first row until the group empties and is never
    // RE-DERIVED at all: only the re-MAX rescan is discriminated there. A
    // signed comparator in the re-MIN rescan would go unnoticed.
    //
    // Two steps, over the same {5, 2^63, 2^64−1}:
    //   A6a. retract 2^63 — a value that is NEITHER extremum. Neither end may
    //        move (mn = 5, mx = 2^64−1) and the count drops to 2. This is the
    //        `__rb == false` path: no rescan runs at all, which is exactly what
    //        must be true when the retracted key is not the cached best.
    //   A6b. on a FRESH handle over the same three rows, retract 5 — the
    //        current MINIMUM — so `mn` must be re-derived over the two
    //        survivors {2^63, 2^64−1}. The answer is 2^63. A rescan comparing
    //        under a SIGNED order answers 2^64−1, whose i64 payload is −1 and
    //        therefore the smallest thing in sight — the same defect
    //        `el_wrap_ord_least`'s header records on the seed side, here on the
    //        rescan side. TWO survivors is the minimum that can see it: with
    //        one survivor left the loop returns it whatever the comparator is.
    let mut h6: UqIncr = uq_incremental();
    let u6: [U; 3] = [U { k: 0i64, v: 5u64 },
                      U { k: 0i64, v: 9223372036854775808u64 },
                      U { k: 0i64, v: 18446744073709551615u64 }];
    match uq_epoch(&mut h6, &u6) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 2i32; } }
    let r6a: [U; 1] = u1(0i64, 9223372036854775808u64);
    match uq_retract(&mut h6, &r6a) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 2i32; } }
    let s6a: Vec<(i64, i64, u64, u64)> = match uq_snapshot(&h6) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 2i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + s6a.len();
    if bad(t, s6a.len() != 1i64) { return 29i32; }
    if bad(t, s6a.get(0i64).1 != 2i64) { return 30i32; }
    if bad(t, s6a.get(0i64).2 != 5u64) { return 31i32; }
    if bad(t, s6a.get(0i64).3 != 18446744073709551615u64) { return 32i32; }

    let mut h7: UqIncr = uq_incremental();
    match uq_epoch(&mut h7, &u6) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 2i32; } }
    let r6b: [U; 1] = u1(0i64, 5u64);
    match uq_retract(&mut h7, &r6b) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 2i32; } }
    let s6b: Vec<(i64, i64, u64, u64)> = match uq_snapshot(&h7) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 2i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + s6b.len();
    if bad(t, s6b.len() != 1i64) { return 33i32; }
    if bad(t, s6b.get(0i64).1 != 2i64) { return 34i32; }
    // THE ASSERTION. 2^63, not 2^64−1.
    if bad(t, s6b.get(0i64).2 != 9223372036854775808u64) { return 35i32; }
    if bad(t, s6b.get(0i64).3 != 18446744073709551615u64) { return 36i32; }
    // …and the batch fn over the two survivors says the same thing, by a fold
    // that shares no map, no seed and no rescan with the path above.
    let b6: [U; 2] = [U { k: 0i64, v: 9223372036854775808u64 },
                      U { k: 0i64, v: 18446744073709551615u64 }];
    let b6v: Vec<(i64, i64, u64, u64)> = match uq(&b6) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 2i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + b6v.len();
    if bad(t, b6v.len() != 1i64) { return 37i32; }
    if bad(t, b6v.get(0i64).2 != s6b.get(0i64).2) { return 38i32; }
    if bad(t, b6v.get(0i64).3 != s6b.get(0i64).3) { return 39i32; }

    // ══ B. MULTIPLICITY — the whole reason the map holds a COUNT ═══════════
    let mut hi: IqIncr = iq_incremental();
    let ib: [I; 3] = [I { k: 0i64, v: 7i64 }, I { k: 0i64, v: 7i64 }, I { k: 0i64, v: 3i64 }];
    match iq_epoch(&mut hi, &ib) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 3i32; } }
    let b0: Vec<(i64, i64, i64, i64, i64)> = match iq_snapshot(&hi) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 3i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + b0.len();
    if bad(t, b0.len() != 1i64) { return 40i32; }
    if bad(t, b0.get(0i64).1 != 3i64 ) { return 41i32; }
    if bad(t, b0.get(0i64).2 != 17i64) { return 42i32; }
    if bad(t, b0.get(0i64).3 != 3i64 ) { return 43i32; }
    if bad(t, b0.get(0i64).4 != 7i64 ) { return 44i32; }

    // retract ONE of the two 7s: `hi` must STILL be 7.
    let ibr: [I; 1] = i1(0i64, 7i64);
    match iq_retract(&mut hi, &ibr) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 3i32; } }
    let b1: Vec<(i64, i64, i64, i64, i64)> = match iq_snapshot(&hi) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 3i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + b1.len();
    if bad(t, b1.len() != 1i64) { return 45i32; }
    if bad(t, b1.get(0i64).1 != 2i64 ) { return 46i32; }
    if bad(t, b1.get(0i64).2 != 10i64) { return 47i32; }
    if bad(t, b1.get(0i64).3 != 3i64 ) { return 48i32; }
    if bad(t, b1.get(0i64).4 != 7i64 ) { return 49i32; }

    // retract the OTHER 7: now the rescan must find 3.
    match iq_retract(&mut hi, &ibr) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 3i32; } }
    let b2: Vec<(i64, i64, i64, i64, i64)> = match iq_snapshot(&hi) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 3i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + b2.len();
    if bad(t, b2.get(0i64).1 != 1i64) { return 50i32; }
    if bad(t, b2.get(0i64).2 != 3i64) { return 51i32; }
    if bad(t, b2.get(0i64).3 != 3i64) { return 52i32; }
    if bad(t, b2.get(0i64).4 != 3i64) { return 53i32; }

    // ══ C. AN EMPTIED GROUP DISAPPEARS AND ITS SIBLING DOES NOT ═══════════
    let mut hc: IqIncr = iq_incremental();
    let ic: [I; 2] = [I { k: 0i64, v: 1i64 }, I { k: 1i64, v: 2i64 }];
    match iq_epoch(&mut hc, &ic) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 4i32; } }
    let icr: [I; 1] = i1(0i64, 1i64);
    match iq_retract(&mut hc, &icr) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 4i32; } }
    let c1: Vec<(i64, i64, i64, i64, i64)> = match iq_snapshot(&hc) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 4i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + c1.len();
    if bad(t, c1.len() != 1i64) { return 54i32; }
    if bad(t, c1.get(0i64).0 != 1i64) { return 55i32; }
    if bad(t, c1.get(0i64).1 != 1i64) { return 56i32; }
    if bad(t, c1.get(0i64).4 != 2i64) { return 57i32; }

    // ══ D. FLOATS — IDENTITY IS THE MAP KEY, ORDER IS THE COMPARATOR ══════
    let mut hf: FqIncr = fq_incremental();
    let fd: [F; 4] = [F { k: 0i64, v: -0.0f64 },
                      F { k: 0i64, v:  0.0f64 },
                      F { k: 0i64, v: f64_from_bits(0x7FF8000000000000u64) },
                      F { k: 0i64, v:  1.0f64 }];
    match fq_epoch(&mut hf, &fd) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 5i32; } }
    let d0: Vec<(i64, f64, f64)> = match fq_snapshot(&hf) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 5i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + d0.len();
    if bad(t, d0.len() != 1i64) { return 58i32; }
    // BITS, not `==`: −0.0 == +0.0 and NaN != NaN, so `==` cannot see either.
    if bad(t, f64_to_bits(d0.get(0i64).1) != 0x8000000000000000u64) { return 59i32; }
    if bad(t, f64_to_bits(d0.get(0i64).2) != 0x7FF8000000000000u64) { return 60i32; }

    // retract −0.0. THE CELL: `lo` must become +0.0, bits all-zero. A map keyed
    // on `f64_data_key` collapses the two zeros onto one key and answers −0.0.
    // ⚠ AND THAT COLLAPSE IS THE DECIDED DESTINATION (2026-08-07, header): when
    // it lands, THIS assertion becomes 0x8000000000000000. Do not "repair" the
    // emitter back to a bitwise key to keep this line green.
    let fr1: [F; 1] = f1(0i64, -0.0f64);
    match fq_retract(&mut hf, &fr1) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 5i32; } }
    let d1: Vec<(i64, f64, f64)> = match fq_snapshot(&hf) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 5i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + d1.len();
    if bad(t, f64_to_bits(d1.get(0i64).1) != 0x0000000000000000u64) { return 61i32; }
    if bad(t, f64_to_bits(d1.get(0i64).2) != 0x7FF8000000000000u64) { return 62i32; }

    // retract the NaN — the rescan's other end: `hi` must become 1.0.
    let fr2: [F; 1] = f1(0i64, f64_from_bits(0x7FF8000000000000u64));
    match fq_retract(&mut hf, &fr2) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 5i32; } }
    let d2: Vec<(i64, f64, f64)> = match fq_snapshot(&hf) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 5i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + d2.len();
    if bad(t, f64_to_bits(d2.get(0i64).2) != 0x3FF0000000000000u64) { return 63i32; }
    if bad(t, f64_to_bits(d2.get(0i64).1) != 0x0000000000000000u64) { return 64i32; }

    // ══ E. RETRACTING A VALUE THIS GROUP NEVER HELD, WITH NO VALUE MAP ═══════
    //
    // ⚠⚠ THIS BLOCK CHANGED ARM ON 2026-08-08, AND THE WITNESS IT USED TO CARRY
    // IS PROVABLY GONE RATHER THAN MERELY MOVED. It read "a u64 SUM DRIVEN BELOW
    // ZERO ERRS, AND SAYS *UNDERFLOW*": insert 1 TWICE (count 2, total 2) and
    // retract ONE row carrying 5 — the count 2 → 1 stays legal, so the group
    // guard does not fire, and 2 − 5 in u64 was `el_subu`'s borrow.
    //
    // `<q>_apply` now carries a FOOTPRINT sensor on the bare scan (see
    // `emit_incremental`): the handle keeps the (key, aggregate-argument) tuples
    // it has actually been handed, in Z-set normal form, and REFUSES a
    // retraction of a footprint it does not hold before any fold runs. `(0,5)`
    // was never inserted, so this call is now `RetractAbsent` — and that is the
    // RIGHT answer for this input: it names the caller's mistake instead of
    // reporting a borrow that only happened because the mistake went undetected.
    //
    // ⚠ AND `el_subu`'s UNDERFLOW IS NOW UNREACHABLE THROUGH `<q>_retract` AT
    // ALL — this is a PROOF, not an observation, because "no test reaches it"
    // and "nothing can reach it" are different claims and only the second
    // licenses retiring a witness. Every stored weight is ≥ 0 (normal form), so
    // a group's u64 total is Σ_f w_f·v_f over the footprints it holds. A
    // retraction is admitted only for a footprint f with |w| ≤ w_f, and it
    // subtracts |w|·v_f ≤ w_f·v_f ≤ total. The subtrahend is bounded by a single
    // term of the sum it is subtracted from, so the borrow cannot occur.
    // (`__s0w` can only LAG the accumulators — the integration runs after the
    // fold and the row returns at the first fault — and lagging over-refuses,
    // which is the safe direction.)
    //
    // SO THE ARITHMETIC WITNESS IS RE-SITED AT ITS OWN SEAM, DIRECTLY, BELOW.
    // `el_subu` had exactly two witnesses in the whole corpus — this block and
    // `wql_incr_retract_three_ways` F2 — and both were routes that can no longer
    // reach it. Deleting them would have left a guard against a u64 wrap (or, as
    // that fixture measured, an "Illegal instruction") with nothing asserting it
    // at all.
    let mut hs: SqIncr = sq_incremental();
    let se: [U; 2] = [U { k: 0i64, v: 1u64 }, U { k: 0i64, v: 1u64 }];
    match sq_epoch(&mut hs, &se) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 6i32; } }
    let sr: [U; 1] = u1(0i64, 5u64);
    match sq_retract(&mut hs, &sr) {
        Result::Ok(_n)  => { return 65i32; }   // a wrap to ≈2^64 would land here
        Result::Err(e1) => {
            match e1 {
                ElError::RetractAbsent => { arm_ok(t); }
                // `Underflow` here means the footprint sensor was removed and
                // `el_subu` caught the same call instead — a DIFFERENT fact
                // reported for the same input, and the exact shape of the
                // regression this block now guards.
                ElError::Underflow => { println!("E WRONG ARM: Underflow — the footprint sensor is gone and el_subu caught the same call"); return 66i32; }
                ElError::Overflow  => { println!("E WRONG ARM: Overflow");  return 66i32; }
                ElError::DivByZero => { println!("E WRONG ARM: DivByZero"); return 66i32; }
                // ⚠ REACHED ONLY IF THE DISPATCH STOPS READING ITS OWN TAG. This
                // call goes through `<q>_retract`, which writes the LITERAL
                // `0i32`, so a `SrcUnknown` here says the emitted `__src` arm
                // compares against something other than the parameter.
                ElError::SrcUnknown    => { return 118i32; }
            }
        }
    }
    // …and `el_subu`'s borrow, asserted where it lives. 2 − 5 over u64.
    match el_subu(2u64, 5u64) {
        Result::Ok(_v)  => { return 67i32; }
        Result::Err(e1b) => {
            match e1b {
                ElError::Underflow     => { arm_ok(t); }
                ElError::RetractAbsent => { println!("E2 WRONG ARM: RetractAbsent"); return 68i32; }
                ElError::Overflow      => { println!("E2 WRONG ARM: Overflow");      return 68i32; }
                ElError::DivByZero     => { println!("E2 WRONG ARM: DivByZero");     return 68i32; }
                ElError::SrcUnknown    => { println!("E2 WRONG ARM: SrcUnknown");    return 68i32; }
            }
        }
    }
    // …and the ADJACENT legal call, because a guard that refuses everything is
    // not a guard: 5 − 2 must still be 3.
    match el_subu(5u64, 2u64) {
        Result::Ok(v1b)  => { if bad(t, v1b != 3u64) { println!("E2: el_subu(5,2) = {}", v1b); return 68i32; } }
        Result::Err(_e1c) => { println!("E2: el_subu(5,2) refused a legal subtraction"); return 68i32; }
    }

    // ══ F. RETRACTING INTO A GROUP THAT NEVER EXISTED ERRS ════════════════
    let mut hf2: IqIncr = iq_incremental();
    let fr: [I; 1] = i1(9i64, 1i64);
    match iq_retract(&mut hf2, &fr) {
        Result::Ok(_n)  => { return 69i32; }
        Result::Err(e2) => {
            match e2 {
                ElError::RetractAbsent => { arm_ok(t); }
                ElError::Underflow  => { return 70i32; }
                ElError::Overflow   => { return 71i32; }
                ElError::DivByZero  => { return 72i32; }
                ElError::SrcUnknown => { return 119i32; }
            }
        }
    }

    // ══ G. RETRACTING THE SAME ROW TWICE DRIVES THE COUNT BELOW ZERO ══════
    let mut hg: IqIncr = iq_incremental();
    let g1: [I; 1] = i1(0i64, 4i64);
    match iq_epoch(&mut hg, &g1) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 8i32; } }
    match iq_retract(&mut hg, &g1) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 8i32; } }
    match iq_retract(&mut hg, &g1) {
        Result::Ok(_n)  => { return 73i32; }
        Result::Err(e3) => {
            match e3 {
                ElError::RetractAbsent => { arm_ok(t); }
                ElError::Underflow  => { return 74i32; }
                ElError::Overflow   => { return 75i32; }
                ElError::DivByZero  => { return 76i32; }
                ElError::SrcUnknown => { return 120i32; }
            }
        }
    }

    // ══ G2. THE ADJACENT SILENT CASE: THE GROUP EXISTS, THE VALUE NEVER DID ═
    // ⚠ THE ONE CELL WHERE NEITHER TIER HAD AN ANSWER, DECIDED THE SAME WAY AND
    // PINNED. Insert (0,3) TWICE, then retract (0,8). The group's count goes
    // 2 → 1, which is legal, so G's guard does not fire; but 8 is a value this
    // group never held, so the map's `remove(&8)` is a NO-OP and the retraction
    // would simply VANISH — an `Ok` describing a multiset the handle never had.
    // The static tier calls that what it is: the caller retracted what was never
    // inserted, `RetractAbsent`, the same name as F and G because it is the same
    // mistake. Neither the batch fn nor the interpreter can arbitrate this
    // (the interpreter's vmap `remove` is equally silent), so it is DECIDED
    // rather than measured, and this assertion is what makes the decision
    // visible to whoever revisits it.
    //
    // ⚠ AND THE `lo`/`hi` CELLS BELOW ARE THE SECOND HALF: after the refusal
    // the group's own answers must be UNCHANGED for the reader to know the
    // refusal is the whole story — this is NOT a claim that `_apply` is
    // transactional (it is not; see the error contract in `emit_incremental`),
    // it is the narrower fact that this particular refusal happens before this
    // particular aggregate's cell moves.
    let mut hv: IqIncr = iq_incremental();
    let v3: [I; 2] = [I { k: 0i64, v: 3i64 }, I { k: 0i64, v: 3i64 }];
    match iq_epoch(&mut hv, &v3) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 9i32; } }
    let v8: [I; 1] = i1(0i64, 8i64);
    match iq_retract(&mut hv, &v8) {
        Result::Ok(_n)  => { return 77i32; }   // a silent `remove` no-op lands here
        Result::Err(e4) => {
            match e4 {
                ElError::RetractAbsent => { arm_ok(t); }
                ElError::Underflow  => { return 78i32; }
                ElError::Overflow   => { return 79i32; }
                ElError::DivByZero  => { return 80i32; }
                ElError::SrcUnknown => { return 121i32; }
            }
        }
    }
    let vv: Vec<(i64, i64, i64, i64, i64)> = match iq_snapshot(&hv) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 9i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + vv.len();
    if bad(t, vv.len() != 1i64) { return 81i32; }
    if bad(t, vv.get(0i64).3 != 3i64) { return 82i32; }   // lo untouched by the refusal
    if bad(t, vv.get(0i64).4 != 3i64) { return 83i32; }   // hi likewise
    // ⚠⚠ THE TWO CELLS THAT ACTUALLY MOVED, AND THEY HAD NO ASSERTION UNTIL
    // 2026-08-08. `lo`/`hi` above belong to the aggregate whose OWN guard
    // refused, so they were never going to move whatever the code did — an
    // assertion about a value the callee would leave alone anyway. The cells the
    // refusal used to CORRUPT are the ones declared BEFORE the `min`, because
    // `#folds` is spliced in AGGREGATE DECLARATION ORDER and the map's guard
    // lives inside the min's own fold element rather than beside the group
    // guards. MEASURED at 360b2761 on exactly this input: the refusal returned
    // `Err(RetractAbsent)` and left c = 1 and t = −2 (6 + (−1)·8) — an `Err` on
    // top of a group holding a total no input produces. That is the
    // "non-transactional `_apply`" half of the contract in `emit_incremental`'s
    // header, DOCUMENTED since P2 and never once asserted where it is
    // observable, which is why it survived.
    //
    // THE DECISION, stated here because this is where a reader meets it:
    // `_apply` stays NON-transactional across a multi-row delta and across a
    // fault raised inside a fold, and it becomes atomic PER DELTA ROW — a row
    // that is REFUSED leaves the handle exactly as it was. So these two cells
    // are now pinned at their pre-call values, and a future slice that makes
    // `_apply` atomic in the wider sense STRENGTHENS this and moves nothing.
    // ⚠ THE PARAMETER CHANNEL, NOT TWO NEW CODES, AND THE HEADER ALREADY SAID
    // WHY: 122/123/124 are the only free numbers under the ceiling, so these
    // share 122 and name themselves on stdout.
    if bad(t, vv.get(0i64).1 != 2i64) { println!("G2: COUNT moved under a refused retract: {}", vv.get(0i64).1); return 122i32; }
    if bad(t, vv.get(0i64).2 != 6i64) { println!("G2: SUM moved under a refused retract: {}", vv.get(0i64).2); return 122i32; }

    // ══ G3. THE SHIPPING DEFECT: A HANDLE WITH NO VALUE-LEVEL STATE AT ALL ═══
    //
    // ⚠⚠ EVERY BLOCK ABOVE THAT REFUSES A NEVER-INSERTED VALUE USES `iq`, WHICH
    // CARRIES A `min` — AND THE `min` IS WHY THEY WORKED. `want_map` builds a
    // value→multiplicity map for a retract-eligible `min`/`max` and for nothing
    // else, so F, G and G2 all lean on the ONE value-level sensor in the handle.
    // Take it away and the surface had no sensor at all. MEASURED at 360b2761,
    // and the two shapes below fail DIFFERENTLY, which is why both are here:
    //
    //   · G3b, `tq` (i64 `sum`): insert (1,10) and (1,5) → the group reads 15;
    //     retract (1,999), never inserted → **Ok(1)**, and the total reads −984.
    //     No error, no name, a public fn returning success on a live handle
    //     holding a number no input produces.
    //   · G3, `sq` (u64 `count` + `sum`): the same input → **Err(Underflow)**,
    //     with the group COUNT already dropped 2 → 1 and the total left at 15.
    //     So this shape does not return `Ok` — it reports the wrong fact (an
    //     arithmetic borrow, when the caller retracted what was never inserted)
    //     and still leaves the handle mutated behind an `Err`. `u64` is the only
    //     reason it is not silent, which is luck rather than a sensor.
    //
    // Nothing fired in either case because the group count went 2 → 1, which is
    // LEGAL, so the group guard is silent, and a `sum`-only or `count`+`sum`
    // query has no second sensor to be silent.
    //
    // ⚠ THE SEEDING IS THE POINT AND IT IS NOT DECORATION. Two rows are inserted
    // FIRST so that a real fold has happened and the group holds 15 before the
    // bad retraction arrives — a handle that refuses cheaply, before doing any
    // work, looks exactly like one that refuses correctly, and 15 is a value no
    // cheap refusal can produce. `sq` is the one query in this file with an
    // aggregate list and NO `min`/`max`; `tq` below is the same statement
    // stripped further, a single `sum` and not even a `count`.
    let mut hs3: SqIncr = sq_incremental();
    let s3: [U; 2] = [U { k: 1i64, v: 10u64 }, U { k: 1i64, v: 5u64 }];
    match sq_epoch(&mut hs3, &s3) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 6i32; } }
    let s3r: [U; 1] = u1(1i64, 999u64);
    match sq_retract(&mut hs3, &s3r) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { println!("G3: sq_retract of a NEVER-INSERTED row returned Ok"); return 123i32; }
        Result::Err(e5) => {
            match e5 {
                ElError::RetractAbsent => { arm_ok(t); }
                ElError::Underflow  => { println!("G3 WRONG ARM: Underflow");  return 123i32; }
                ElError::Overflow   => { println!("G3 WRONG ARM: Overflow");   return 123i32; }
                ElError::DivByZero  => { println!("G3 WRONG ARM: DivByZero");  return 123i32; }
                ElError::SrcUnknown => { println!("G3 WRONG ARM: SrcUnknown"); return 123i32; }
            }
        }
    }
    let s3v: Vec<(i64, i64, u64)> = match sq_snapshot(&hs3) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 6i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + s3v.len();
    if bad(t, s3v.len() != 1i64)         { println!("G3: rows {}", s3v.len());          return 124i32; }
    if bad(t, s3v.get(0i64).1 != 2i64)   { println!("G3: sq COUNT {}", s3v.get(0i64).1); return 124i32; }
    if bad(t, s3v.get(0i64).2 != 15u64)  { println!("G3: sq SUM {}", s3v.get(0i64).2);   return 124i32; }

    // G3b. THE SAME STATEMENT AT ITS MINIMUM — one `sum`, over i64, and nothing
    // else on the handle but the group key and the group count. This is the
    // brief's own repro, verbatim: it is the shape with the fewest cells that
    // can still hold a wrong number, and at 360b2761 it answered `Ok(1)` and
    // then read −984.
    let mut ht3: TqIncr = tq_incremental();
    let t3: [I; 2] = [I { k: 1i64, v: 10i64 }, I { k: 1i64, v: 5i64 }];
    match tq_epoch(&mut ht3, &t3) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 6i32; } }
    let t3r: [I; 1] = i1(1i64, 999i64);
    match tq_retract(&mut ht3, &t3r) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { println!("G3b: tq_retract of a NEVER-INSERTED row returned Ok"); return 123i32; }
        Result::Err(e6) => {
            match e6 {
                ElError::RetractAbsent => { arm_ok(t); }
                ElError::Underflow  => { println!("G3b WRONG ARM: Underflow");  return 123i32; }
                ElError::Overflow   => { println!("G3b WRONG ARM: Overflow");   return 123i32; }
                ElError::DivByZero  => { println!("G3b WRONG ARM: DivByZero");  return 123i32; }
                ElError::SrcUnknown => { println!("G3b WRONG ARM: SrcUnknown"); return 123i32; }
            }
        }
    }
    let t3v: Vec<(i64, i64)> = match tq_snapshot(&ht3) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 6i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + t3v.len();
    if bad(t, t3v.len() != 1i64)       { println!("G3b: rows {}", t3v.len());          return 124i32; }
    if bad(t, t3v.get(0i64).1 != 15i64) { println!("G3b: tq SUM {}", t3v.get(0i64).1); return 124i32; }

    // G3c. AND THE CONTROL, because a sensor that refuses EVERYTHING refuses
    // correctly on every input above while destroying the capability. Retract a
    // row that WAS inserted: it must succeed and the total must fall to 10.
    let t3ok: [I; 1] = i1(1i64, 5i64);
    match tq_retract(&mut ht3, &t3ok) {
        Result::Ok(_n)  => { arm_ok(t); }
        Result::Err(_e) => { println!("G3c: the footprint sensor refused a retraction of a row that WAS inserted"); return 124i32; }
    }
    let t3w: Vec<(i64, i64)> = match tq_snapshot(&ht3) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 6i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + t3w.len();
    if bad(t, t3w.get(0i64).1 != 10i64) { println!("G3c: tq SUM after a LEGAL retract {}", t3w.get(0i64).1); return 124i32; }

    // ══ H. GROUP ORDER AFTER A GROUP EMPTIES AND COMES BACK ═══════════════
    // ⚠ AN OPEN SURFACE QUESTION, PINNED AS MEASURED RATHER THAN DECIDED.
    // `__gk` slots are appended on first sight and never removed (the dynamic
    // engine's slots behave identically, so it cannot arbitrate this — the case
    // the brief warns about, where all sides agreeing is not evidence). Insert
    // (0,1) then (1,2); retract (0,1), emptying g0; insert (0,9). The handle's
    // slot order is still [g0, g1] — FIRST OCCURRENCE OVER THE WHOLE HISTORY —
    // while `<q>()` over the survivors LISTED IN ARRIVAL ORDER, [(1,2), (0,9)],
    // answers [g1, g0].
    //
    // Both are defensible and the choice is a CONTRACT, not an emitter detail:
    // (a) restate the metamorphic law as multiset equality once any retraction
    // has occurred; (b) declare slot order the contract and say that `<q>()` and
    // `<q>_snapshot` may differ in ROW ORDER after one; (c) compact slots on
    // empty. This block takes none of them — it pins what the code does today so
    // that a future slice changing it has to change a fixture and say so.
    // `wql_incr_static_three_ways` block E's "same rows, same order" law is an
    // INSERT-ONLY law and is left exactly as it is.
    let mut hh: IqIncr = iq_incremental();
    let ih: [I; 2] = [I { k: 0i64, v: 1i64 }, I { k: 1i64, v: 2i64 }];
    match iq_epoch(&mut hh, &ih) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; } }
    let ihr: [I; 1] = i1(0i64, 1i64);
    match iq_retract(&mut hh, &ihr) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; } }
    let ihb: [I; 1] = i1(0i64, 9i64);
    match iq_epoch(&mut hh, &ihb) { Result::Ok(_n) => {} Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; } }
    let hv: Vec<(i64, i64, i64, i64, i64)> = match iq_snapshot(&hh) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + hv.len();
    if bad(t, hv.len() != 2i64) { return 84i32; }
    if bad(t, hv.get(0i64).0 != 0i64) { return 85i32; }   // g0 keeps its ORIGINAL slot
    if bad(t, hv.get(0i64).4 != 9i64) { return 86i32; }
    if bad(t, hv.get(1i64).0 != 1i64) { return 87i32; }
    // …and the batch fn over the survivors in ARRIVAL order disagrees on ORDER
    // while agreeing on every row. That is the fork, stated as an assertion.
    let hb: [I; 2] = [I { k: 1i64, v: 2i64 }, I { k: 0i64, v: 9i64 }];
    let hbv: Vec<(i64, i64, i64, i64, i64)> = match iq(&hb) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + hbv.len();
    if bad(t, hbv.len() != 2i64) { return 88i32; }
    if bad(t, hbv.get(0i64).0 != 1i64) { return 89i32; }
    if bad(t, hbv.get(1i64).0 != 0i64) { return 90i32; }
    if bad(t, hbv.get(1i64).4 != hv.get(0i64).4) { return 91i32; }   // same ROW, other slot

    // ══ (ii) THE STATIC BATCH FN OVER THE SURVIVING MULTISET ══════════════
    // A after two retractions is the multiset {5}; B after one retraction is
    // {7, 3}. The batch fold shares no seed, no group-creation step and no fold
    // with the retract path.
    let ba: [U; 1] = [U { k: 0i64, v: 5u64 }];
    let bav: Vec<(i64, i64, u64, u64)> = match uq(&ba) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 11i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + bav.len();
    if bad(t, bav.len() != 1i64) { return 92i32; }
    if bad(t, bav.get(0i64).2 != a2.get(0i64).2) { return 93i32; }
    if bad(t, bav.get(0i64).3 != a2.get(0i64).3) { return 94i32; }
    let bb: [I; 2] = [I { k: 0i64, v: 7i64 }, I { k: 0i64, v: 3i64 }];
    let bbv: Vec<(i64, i64, i64, i64, i64)> = match iq(&bb) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 11i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + bbv.len();
    if bad(t, bbv.len() != 1i64) { return 95i32; }
    if bad(t, bbv.get(0i64).1 != b1.get(0i64).1) { return 96i32; }
    if bad(t, bbv.get(0i64).2 != b1.get(0i64).2) { return 97i32; }
    if bad(t, bbv.get(0i64).3 != b1.get(0i64).3) { return 98i32; }
    if bad(t, bbv.get(0i64).4 != b1.get(0i64).4) { return 99i32; }
    // C's survivor, likewise.
    let bc: [I; 1] = [I { k: 1i64, v: 2i64 }];
    let bcv: Vec<(i64, i64, i64, i64, i64)> = match iq(&bc) { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 11i32; } };
    t.rows = t.rows + bcv.len();
    if bad(t, bcv.len() != c1.len()) { return 100i32; }
    if bad(t, bcv.get(0i64).0 != c1.get(0i64).0) { return 101i32; }
    if bad(t, bcv.get(0i64).4 != c1.get(0i64).4) { return 102i32; }

    // ══ (iii) THE DYNAMIC INTERPRETER, DRIVEN WITH A w = −1 BATCH ═════════
    // `incremental()` BASE-FILLS from the bound source, so the engine starts at
    // the same multiset the static handle reached by its first epoch; the
    // `ZBatch` then retracts the same row. `get_i64` reads a u64 cell's BITS, so
    // 2^63 comes back as −9223372036854775808 — the constant is the same number.
    let d: Writ = writ_new(65536i64);
    let c: SchemaCatalog = cat();
    let ar: &mut WArray<WAny> = d.array(4i64);
    let h_big: WAny = mkU(&d, 0i64, 18446744073709551615u64);
    ar.push(mkU(&d, 0i64, 5u64));
    ar.push(mkU(&d, 0i64, 9223372036854775808u64));
    ar.push(h_big);
    let mut e: QEnv = QEnv::new();
    e.bind_source("ss", WAny::ref_to(ar as *const WArray<WAny> as *const u8));
    let dq: Query = match Query::compile(
        "from ss s group by s.k aggregate c = count(), mn = min(s.v), mx = max(s.v) select (key, c, mn, mx)", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v)   => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 12i32; } };
    let mut eng: IncrJoin = match dq.incremental(&e) {
        Result::Ok(v)   => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 12i32; } };
    if bad(t, eng.had_error()) { return 103i32; }
    let ds0: QRows = eng.snapshot();
    t.rows = t.rows + ds0.row_count();
    if bad(t, ds0.row_count() != 1i64) { return 104i32; }
    let mut z: ZBatch = ZBatch::new();
    let empty: ZBatch = ZBatch::new();
    z.push(h_big, -1i64);
    eng.epoch(&z, &empty);
    if bad(t, eng.had_error()) { return 105i32; }
    let ds: QRows = eng.snapshot();
    t.rows = t.rows + ds.row_count();
    if bad(t, ds.row_count() != 1i64) { return 106i32; }
    // count = 2, min = 5, max = 2^63 (bits −9223372036854775808)
    if bad(t, ds.get_i64(0i64, 1i64) != 2i64) { return 107i32; }
    if bad(t, ds.get_i64(0i64, 2i64) != 5i64) { return 108i32; }
    if bad(t, ds.get_i64(0i64, 3i64) != -9223372036854775808i64) { return 109i32; }
    // …and that is bit-for-bit what the static handle answered in block A.
    if bad(t, ds.get_i64(0i64, 3i64) != (a1.get(0i64).3 as i64)) { return 110i32; }
    if bad(t, ds.get_i64(0i64, 1i64) != a1.get(0i64).1) { return 111i32; }

    // The MULTIPLICITY cell, of the interpreter: 7 twice and 3, retract one 7.
    let ar2: &mut WArray<WAny> = d.array(4i64);
    let h7: WAny = mkI(&d, 0i64, 7i64);
    ar2.push(h7);
    ar2.push(mkI(&d, 0i64, 7i64));
    ar2.push(mkI(&d, 0i64, 3i64));
    let mut e2: QEnv = QEnv::new();
    e2.bind_source("ss", WAny::ref_to(ar2 as *const WArray<WAny> as *const u8));
    let dq2: Query = match Query::compile(
        "from ss s group by s.k aggregate c = count(), t = sum(s.v), lo = min(s.v), hi = max(s.v) select (key, c, t, lo, hi)", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v)   => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 12i32; } };
    let mut eng2: IncrJoin = match dq2.incremental(&e2) {
        Result::Ok(v)   => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 12i32; } };
    let mut z2: ZBatch = ZBatch::new();
    z2.push(h7, -1i64);
    eng2.epoch(&z2, &empty);
    if bad(t, eng2.had_error()) { return 112i32; }
    let ds2: QRows = eng2.snapshot();
    t.rows = t.rows + ds2.row_count();
    if bad(t, ds2.row_count() != 1i64) { return 113i32; }
    if bad(t, ds2.get_i64(0i64, 1i64) != 2i64 ) { return 114i32; }
    if bad(t, ds2.get_i64(0i64, 2i64) != 10i64) { return 115i32; }
    if bad(t, ds2.get_i64(0i64, 3i64) != 3i64 ) { return 116i32; }
    if bad(t, ds2.get_i64(0i64, 4i64) != 7i64 ) { return 117i32; }

    return 0i32;
}
